# Copy to .env.local and fill. NEVER commit the filled version.
#
# Next.js reads .env.local automatically. Only NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables reach the
# browser — anything without that prefix stays server-side. Nothing secret ever
# gets the prefix: the Supabase SERVICE key, JWT_SECRET, and HMAC_SECRET live in
# apps/api/.env and must never appear in this file.
#
# This list is short because the web app is still a shell. It grows one slice at
# a time; add a variable here in the same commit as the code that reads it.

# --- API ---
# Where apiFetch sends everything. Must include the /v1 prefix — the API serves
# every route under it, health included (main.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3001/v1

# --- Supabase ---
# Deliberately absent. DECIDED 2026-08-18: attorney login proxies through the
# API, so the browser never talks to Supabase and needs no Supabase env at all.
#
# Two reasons it went that way. CLAUDE.md fixes the access token in memory and
# never localStorage, and the Supabase browser SDK persists sessions to
# localStorage by default — direct-to-Supabase means either breaking that rule
# or fighting the library with a custom storage adapter. And a browser-to-
# Supabase channel is a second data path that bypasses the matter-access guard
# and the access_log, which exist precisely because read access to privileged
# material matters as much as writes.
#
# So: POST /v1/auth/login proxies to Supabase, the refresh token comes back as
# an httpOnly cookie the browser cannot read, and the access token lives in the
# Zustand store in memory. apiFetch owns the whole lifecycle (06 Part 6).
#
# 05 §L2 still describes the frontend calling Supabase's refresh endpoint
# directly. That is the stale reading; see log/decision-log.md.
